$DIR/main.p:17:20: channel element type Job is not sendable: it contains shared[int], whose count is not atomic, so it cannot cross tasks; use synced[T] (spec section 19.2)
    A value that crosses tasks, as the argument or receiver of a task statement
    or as the element of a channel, must be sendable. This type holds a
    shared[T], whose reference count is changed with ordinary arithmetic, so two
    tasks copying or dropping references at once would corrupt it. That holds
    through pointers, slices, maps and interfaces too: two tasks that reach one
    struct through a pointer could both copy the shared[T] out of it. Starting a
    method of a shared box with a task statement (task z.Serve()) hands the box
    itself to the new task, so it needs a synced box as well.
    Use synced[T], which counts atomically.
    Go's garbage collector makes every pointer safe to share. Plutonium counts
    shared references without atomic instructions, which is only correct on one
    task.
    Count the references atomically:
        type Job struct {
            data synced[Buffer]
        }

        jobs := make(chan Job, 16)
    (see "Sendable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.8)
    (run "pluto explain E2010" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:21:12: &x is passed to a task statement, but x lives in this function's frame, which the receiving task may outlive (spec section 18.3); allocate it with new, whose owned[T] then moves to the task, or make it a package-level variable
    This value points into the current function's stack frame: the address of a
    local variable or of part of one, a slice of a local array, or the address
    of a composite literal. A task statement or a channel send hands it to
    another task, which may still use it after this function has returned and
    its frame is gone.
    Start the work with a sync.Scope instead: it waits for the tasks it starts
    before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables. Or allocate
    the variable with new, so that it outlives the frame, or make it a
    package-level variable.
    Go moves such variables to the heap without saying so. Plutonium never
    allocates behind the program's back, so a local stays in its frame.
    Start the work with a sync.Scope, which waits for it before its block ends:
        var s sync.Scope
        s.Task(func() { worker(&state) })
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2011" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:24:11: buf[:] is passed to a task statement, but it is a slice of buf, which lives in this function's frame and which the receiving task may outlive (spec section 18.3)
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2011" for more)

$DIR/main.p:27:7: task statement calls a pointer method on c, a variable in this function's frame, which the task may outlive (spec section 18.3); make it a package-level variable, or give the task a function that takes an owner
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2011" for more)

$DIR/main.p:30:10: &x is sent on a channel, but x lives in this function's frame, which the receiving task may outlive (spec section 18.3); allocate it with new, whose owned[T] then moves to the task, or make it a package-level variable
